Economics]\n- Fed funds target range: 3.50\u20133.75%, held for 5th consecutive meeting, July\n  2026; markets assign ~1-in-3 probability of a HIKE [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics]\n- Fed stance: NEUTRAL-to-TIGHTENING (not easing)\n- Equity risk premium (ERP): ~4.75% assumed for large-cap [Assumption \u2014\n  Damodaran-style mature-market implied ERP range 4.5\u20135.0%]\n- Term premium: 10Y (4.71%) \u2212 EFFR (~3.60%) \u2248 +1.1%, steep, normal\n- Inflation: not re-established by search \u2014 [Unconfirmed]. The Fed holding\n  with hike risk priced suggests inflation is not collapsing.\n- Dollar: level not re-established \u2014 [Unconfirmed]. MA derives ~60% of\n  revenue outside the U.S., so DXY is a real earnings variable.\n- Credit spreads: not re-established \u2014 [Unconfirmed], assumed benign given the\n  Fed describe market as \"in a good place\" [Estimate]\n\n1-2. Regime probability distribution (must sum 100%):\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth):   15%\n- Reflation (high rates + growth):   55%\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 20%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth):   10%\nBasis: 10Y at 4.71% rules out \"low rates\" for any regime; growth is still\npositive (MA volumes +13\u201316%), so Reflation is the modal regime. Hike risk\nelevates Stagflation odds; no recession signals present yet.\n\n1-3. Macro sensitivity (5 axes, each \u22123 to +3):\n- Interest rate sensitivity: \u22121 (not a lender; slower consumption is the\n  transmission channel)\n- Dollar sensitivity:       \u22121 (international revenue translation + cross-border)\n- Liquidity sensitivity:    +1 (payment volumes track asset/transaction liquidity)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: +1 (non-discretionary bias, but travel/retail are\n  cyclical)\n- Commodity sensitivity:    0 (no direct input; oil only via consumer wallet)\n\n1-4. Macro alignment:\nExpected macro impact = 0.15\u00d7(+2) + 0.55\u00d7(+1) + 0.20\u00d7(\u22122) + 0.10\u00d7(\u22123)\n= +0.15 \u2192 NEUTRAL (no conviction adjustment, no extra entry discount required).\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN  [Layer 1.5]\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n1.5-1. Base rates applied (reference base rates stated in framework):\n- Quality Compounders at fortress scale surviving 5 years: ~90%\n- Maintaining 10%+ EPS CAGR for 5 years (large-cap): ~55%\n- Duopoly payments networks retaining effective pricing power for 5 years:\n  ~65\u201370% (V/MA combined share still dominant; stablecoin/CBDC substitution\n  remains small but is rising)\n1.5-2. Deviation from base rate: My 10-year revenue CAGR forecast is ~11%\n(2026\u20132035), above the ~55% base rate for large-cap compounding. Justification\n(3+ company-specific): (1) VAS/services now >40% of revenue and growing ~20\u201322%\nper year \u2014 faster than core network; (2) secular cash-to-card conversion and\ncommercial/new-flows expansion; (3) buybacks reduce share count \u22122.3%/yr,\nmechanically boosting per-share compounding; (4) fortress moat with pricing\npower validated by 57.6% operating margin. I judge the justification SUFFICIENT\nbut the deviation is why my confidence interval is wide.\n1.5-3. Base-rate shock check: Success probability implicit in my base case is\n~70% (weighted across scenarios). vs. the 55% base rate for 10%+ EPS CAGR \u2192\nratio 1.27x < 1.5x; no red flag triggered. For the 20%+ VAS growth leg, my\nsuccess probability (~60%) is ~1.3x base rates for platform-transition success\n(~30\u201350%); under the 1.5x threshold.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY  [Layer 2]\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\