xpected Value (EV, probability-weighted): $584\nMargin of Safety at $580.63: -0.3% on Base IV; +0.6% on EV \u2192 NONE on Base, LIMITED on EV\nConviction: 7/15 + 0.5 macro = 7.5/15 \u2192 MEDIUM-HIGH, capped position.\nRecommendation: HOLD / ACCUMULATE BELOW $525 (Fair Value band). No entry at T0.\n12-mo price target (fair, not catalyst-chased): $585\u2013$630.\nDuration: 3\u20135 year thesis; market horizon ~7 years.\n\nSizing: Maximum 6.0% initial (vs. guideline 8% for High conviction, 10% for Very High).\nReason: Conviction 7.5 (not High), Macro alignment Neutral-to-Mildly-Negative,\nvaluation at ~1.01\u00d7 Base IV, and correlation-adjusted portfolio risk. Re-allocate in\ntranches: 30% at 520\u2013549, 40% at 480\u2013519, 30% at 445\u2013479 (see Layer 5).\nIf no entry, hold cash; do not force the position.\n\nKey data reliability flags:\n\u2022 Revenue, OI, Net Income, OCF, Capex, D&A, SBC [Actual] \u2014 SEC filings (Sections 3\u20136).\n\u2022 FCF column in pack is overstated: capex excludes capitalized software [Actual\u2014disclosed;\n  vendor capex $1.215B vs filed P&E capex $0.489B, 2.48\u00d7]. I use an adjusted capex\n  of ~$0.9B for FY2025 for owner-earnings purposes [Estimate].\n\u2022 The research brief's \"FY2026 EPS $14.00\" and \"FCF $1.19B\" figures are INCONSISTENT\n  with actual data (Q2'26 adjusted EPS $5.04; FY2025 FCF > $17B) and are disregarded\n  as transcription/model errors. [Estimate\u2014flagged]\n\u2022 Prior FY revenue, OI, NI, OCF, D&A series [Actual\u2014SEC companyfacts 2014\u20132025].\n\u2022 Beta 0.735 [Aggregator\u2014unverified window; used only as sanity check].\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 LAYER 0. ENTERPRISE CLASSIFICATION & MODEL SELECTION\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 0-1. Archetype Determination:\nArchetype B \u2014 QUALITY COMPOUNDER.\nReasons:\n\u2022 ROIC > WACC consistently for 10+ years (GAAP ROIC ~40%+; adjusted ROIC ~35%).\n\u2022 Steady 15\u201317% revenue growth with 55%+ operating margins (12-yr median 53.7%).\n\u2022 High return on incremental capital; negative working capital (float business).\n\u2022 Monopoly-like network effects with durable switching costs.\n\nStep 0-2. Valuation Engine:\nENGINE 1 \u2014 Traditional DCF + Multiples, applied through Owner Earnings (Buffett\nparallel) and capped/anchored by absolute multiples, per Layer 3.\nWhy Engine 1: The company is a fee-for-service tollbooth with stable, visible\neconomics; not cycle-dominated, not option-led, not binary. An options-based engine\n(Engine 4) would misprice an already-mature platform; a cycle-adjusted engine\n(Engine 2) is irrelevant for a business with 12-yr revenue CAGR of 16.5%.\n\nStep 0-3. Archetype Transition Status: NOT IN TRANSITION.\nEvidence: revenue growth accelerating (FY25 +16.4% vs FY24 +12.2%), margins still\nexpanding (+50 bps), no new capex-heavy model emerging (capex/discrete 1.5% of\nrevenue [Actual\u2014field]), no dividend cut, no margin deterioration.\nAlso note: The company is *adding* VAS (~40% of revenue [Actual\u2014Q1'26 company\ndisclosure]) but that is not a transition; it is a margin-accretive mix shift.\n\nStep 0-4. Justification:\n\u2022 Why this archetype? 3 reasons: (i) 12 years of uninterrupted GAAP profitability;\n(ii) OCF \u2265 90% of net income in 10 of 12 years, i.e., high earnings quality;\n(iii) ROIC > 25% every year [Actual\u2014computed from filed data].\n\u2022 Why Engine 1? Because cash flows are forecastable with low variance: the\n  5-year forward revenue path is anchored at 10\u201312% growth (company guidance),\n  and VAS creates software-like recurring revenue.\n\u2022 Errors if Engine 4 used: It would assign a massive value to optionality (Agentic\n  Commerce, CBDC, BVNK) that is not yet monetized and would inflate IV by ~$200\u2013300.\n\u2022 Errors if Engine 2 used: It would compress normalized earnings artificially and\n  understate compounding quality.\n\u2022 Transition risk: none detected. Conviction 7.5, no -0.5 step.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 LAYER 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 1-1. Macro Variable Check [All sourced, tagged]:\n\u2022 10Y Treasury: 4.70% (Aug 24, 2026) [Actual\u2014Tradeweb via CNBC/FRED]. Up ~44bps YoY.\n\u2022 Real 10Y: ~1.7% (4.70% nominal - ~3.0% breakeven [Estimate\u2014derived from TIPS\n  series in FRED H.15]) [Estimate].\n\u2022 DXY: ~104 [Unconfirmed\u2014model does not hold DXY; inferred from Fed/HY context].\n\u2022 Fed stance: Neutral-to-Easing-skeptical. FOMC on hold at 3.50\u20133.75% [Actual\u2014FOMC\n  July 2026]; market prices only ~1 cut in 2026 [Estimate\u2014deriv.me].\n\u2022 Global liquidity: Liquidity tightening bias; HY OAS 2.75% [Actual\u2014FRED Aug 20,\n  2026] vs long-run 5.20%: abundant risk appetite but repricing at the long end.\n\u2022 Economic cycle: Late expansion / early slowdown \u2014 inflation above target,\n  unemployment low, consumers stretched (see web brief, Section 5).\n\u2022 Inflation: Sticky ~3.0% core [Estimate\u2014web brief & Fed commentary].\n\u2022 Credit spreads: 275\u2013320bps HY [Actual\u2014FRED], tight; credit conditions\n  historically permissive.\n\nStep 1-2. Regime Classification:\n\u2022 Goldilocks (Low rates + growth): 20%\n\u2022 Reflation (High rates + growth): 45%\n\u2022 Stagflation (High rates + low growth): 25%\n\u2022 Recession (Low rates + low growth): 10%\nTotal: 100%.\nBasis: 10Y at 4.70% (not low), real growth still positive, core inflation ~3%,\nnot yet recessionary. Reflation is the modal regime; stagflation is the second-\nmost-likely because of the persistent inflation floor.\n\nStep 1-3. Enterprise/Sector Macro Sensitivity (each -3 to +3):\n\u2022 Interest-rate sensitivity: -1 (network fee model is volume-driven, not\n  interest-rate-driven; higher rates delay consumer credit growth but support\n  bank economics).\n\u2022 Dollar sensitivity: -2 (20%+ of revenue from cross-border; strong USD\n  suppresses international GDV and travel spend).\n\u2022 Liquidity sensitivity: +1 (payments volume rises with liquidity/credit\n  availability).\n\u2022 Economic-cycle sensitivity: -1 (payment volumes are cyclical but consumer\n  staples-like in the short run; historical resilience is high).\n\u2022 Commodity sensitivity: 0 (economy-wide only; fuel price impacts debit\n  switching volumes but not margin structure).\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment:\nRegime-weighted expected macro impact =\n0.20*(+1) + 0.45*(0.5) + 0.25*(-1.5) + 0.10*(-2.5) \u2248 0.2 + 0.225 - 0.375 - 0.25\n= -0.20 \u2192 NEUTRAL-to-MILD HEADWIND.\nMacro rule: No conviction step-up; no step-down because headwind is weak.\nSplit-entry strengthened by default (see Layer 5).\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 LAYER 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base-rate inquiry for a Quality Compounder with 12 years of history:\n\u2022 5-yr survival probability of a company with this archetype:\n  ~90% [Estimate\u2014base rate for profitable, diversified network platforms is\n  materially above the 40\u201360% base rate for pre-revenue Engine-4 names].\n\u2022 Probability of sustaining 10%+ revenue growth for 5 more years:\n  ~60% [Estimate\u2014payments industry 10-yr history: both V and MA have compound\n  growth > 10%, but the base rate for a large-cap company growing > GDP+5% for\n  5 years is ~35\u201340%].\n\u2022 Probability of maintaining ROIC > 20% for 5 years: ~75% [Estimate].\n\u2022 Base rate for large-cap platform M&A > $10B achieving ROIC>WACC: 25\u201335%\n  [Actual\u2014M&A literature; applied to BVNK and smaller deals].\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate: The analysis assumes 60% probability of\n> 10% revenue CAGR. The deviation of my bull thesis from the 60% base rate is\njustified by three company-specific factors:\n(i) VAS at ~40% of revenue growing +18% CN [Actual\u2014Q1'26 disclosure] \u2014 a\nsoftware-like second engine that did not exist 5 years ago;\n(ii) switching and tokenization (>40% token penetration [Actual\u2014Q2'26 transcript])\nraise pricing and retention independently of GDP;\n(iii) structural shift from cash to digital in emerging markets: 230M net new\ncards in 12 months [Actual\u2014Q2'26 transcript], a durable 5\u20136% card-growth tail.\nThese justify an elevated long-term growth path, not a hockey-stick.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base rate shock check:\nSuccess probability used in my bull case = 30% vs. base rate of ~60% \u2192 0.5\u00d7 base\nrate, no red flag.\nIndividual scenario probabilities: Bull 25%, Base 50%, Bear 25% \u2192 all within 1\u00d7\nbase-rate bounds. No adjustment to scenario probabilities required.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 LAYER 2. BUSINESS QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding:\nMastercard operates the world's second-largest open-loop payment network,\nconnecting 3.7B cards [Actual\u2014Q2'26] to ~100M+ merchants across ~210 countries.\nIt earns fees per transaction (switching, cross-border, data/security services)\nrather than interest income, so it carries no credit risk. Value-Added Services\n(cyber/intelligence, consulting, data analytics, identity, open banking) now\ngenerate ~40% of net revenue [Actual\u2014Q1'26] and are network-agnostic.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Assessment (0\u20133 each):\n\u2022 Intangible/Regulatory (brand, \"honor all cards\" rule): 3\n\u2022 Switching costs (issuers, acquirers, merchants): 3\n\u2022 Network effects (two-sided): 3\n\u2022 Cost advantage (infrastructure scale): 2\n\u2022 Economies of scale: 3\n\u2022 Data moat (transaction-level data): 3\n\u2022 Regulatory barriers (licensed schemes): 2\nTotal: 19/21 \u2192 FORTRESS. Moat direction: WIDENING (VAS network-agnostic share\ngains; tokenization >40% vs. 30% prior year [Actual\u2014Q2'26]; contactless 80%\npenetration +5pts [Actual]).\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification: STALWART-to-FAST-GROWER (transitioning to\nStalwart as base grows). Checkpoints: EPS growth 2023\u20132025 = +15.0%, +15.0%,\n+16.3% [Actual\u2014filed, computed]; FY26 adj EPS +19% CN in H1'26 [Actual\u2014Q2'26];\nnot a cyclical, not a turnaround, not an asset play.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard (all [Actual] unless noted):\n\u2022 Revenue 3-yr CAGR (2022\u20132025): 13.8% | last 4Q (Q3'25\u2013Q2'26): $35.08B vs\n  $30.09B prior 4Q \u2192 +16.6% [Actual\u2014computed from filed data].\n\u2022 Operating margin (FY25): 57.6% vs 3-yr avg 54.2% [Actual].\n\u2022 EPS growth FY23/FY24/FY25: +15.0%/+15.0%/+16.3% [Actual\u2014computed].\n\u2022 ROIC (GAAP, NOPAT / (debt+equity) avg): ~40% [Estimate].\n\u2022 FCF conversion (FCF/NI): FY25 = 17.16/14.97 = 115% [Actual\u2014but note capex\n  understatement) \u2192 Adjusted ~105% [Estimate].\n\u2022 Net debt/EBITDA: (18.25 + 0.83 - 10.57) / 18.90 = 0.44x [Estimate\u2014my definition].\n\u2022 Interest coverage: EBIT 18.90 / interest 0.68 = 27.8x [Actual].\n\u2022 Net cash vs. net debt: Net debt ~$8.5B [Estimate\u2014my debt components: LT debt\n  18.25 + current portion 0.75 + leases 0.83 - cash 10.57; marketable\n  securities not held [Unconfirmed] \u2192 may be net cash if securities ~$8B, as\n  on prior filings].\nJudgment: EXCELLENT.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (0\u20133 each):\n1. Buyback efficiency: 3 (aggressive buybacks below mid-cycle P/E; FY25 net share\n   issuance -2.3% [Actual]; Q2'26 $4.9B repurchases + $14B new authorization\n   [Actual\u2014earnings call]).\n2. M&A track record: 2 (VocaLink, Ethoca, Dynamic Yield, Finicity \u2014 but mixed\n   large-deal ROIC; BVNK unclear).\n3. Capex efficiency: 3 (incremental ROIC ~30\u201340% [Estimate]).\n4. Rationality of mix: 3 (reinvestment (VAS, Agentic Commerce, UAE switching)\n   aligned with growth; dividends + buyback well-balanced).\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3 (consistently guided \"high end of low double digits\" and\n   delivered 14\u201317% CN revenue; Q1'26 \"April trends softened\" but Q2 re-\n   accelerated [Actual\u2014Q1'26 transcript via investing.com]).\nInsider bonus: Management ownership 0.11% [Actual] \u2192 0 pts; no insider cluster\nbuying [Unconfirmed\u2014none disclosed]; large insider net sell in last 6mo: none\ndisclosed [Unconfirmed]. \u2192 Total 14/15.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 LAYER 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization (Engine 3-by-analogy):\nGAAP operating income FY25: $18.897B [Actual].\nR&D (not separately disclosed in pack; engineering/product development \u2014 a large\nfraction of SG&A; I estimate R&D-equivalent ~ $2.4B [Assumption\u201435% of SG&A,\nconsistent with Mastercard's engineering focus]).\nIf capitalized and amortized over 4 years (straight-line, starting FY22):\nAdd back FY25 R&D: +$2.4B; subtract FY25 amortization of prior capitalized R&D:\n- $1.4B [Assumption\u2014cumulative intangible base ~$5.8B, straight-line 4y].\nAdjusted OI: ~$19.9B [Estimate].\nAdjusted invested capital: ~$8.0B (book equity $7.75B) + unamortized R&D ~6.2B\n[Estimate] \u2192 implied Adjusted ROIC = 19.9 \u00d7 (1\u20130.20) / 14.2 \u2248 112% [Estimate].\nThat is not a meaningful statistic \u2014 it only proves that the GAAP ROIC already\nunderstates the economics. GAAP ROIC ~40% [Estimate]; gap > 5pts explained by\nR&D and brand expensing. Conclusion: economic ROIC is far above GAAP ROIC.\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC proper treatment:\nFY25 SBC: $0.597B [Actual] \u2014 1.8% of revenue. GAAP FCF FY25: $17.16B.\nAdjusted FCF (OCF - SBC - adjusted capex) = 17.65 - 0.60 - 0.90 = $16.15B\n[Estimate].\nSBC is modest, not a hidden compensation problem; the company's buyback program\nprovided net -2.3% share count per year, offsetting dilution and more [Actual].\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating lease capitalization:\nRoU assets FY25: $0.83B [Actual\u2014op. lease line in 6B]. Debt-adjusted EBITDA:\n18.90 + 0.68 + 0.83 \u2248 20.4; Adjusted debt = 18.25 + 0.75 + 0.83 = 19.83.\nAdjusted net debt = 19.83\u201310.57 = $9.26B [Estimate]. Not material.\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/Data asset:\nConsumer brand: Mastercard brand value \u2248 $30\u201335B [Estimate\u2014Interbrand-style],\nbut brand does not generate separable cash flows; not added to IV.\nData asset (transaction-level behavioral dataset): not separately valued;\ncaptured in VAS revenue growth.\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: as in 2.5-1. Wide gap vs. GAAP ~72pts.\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings FY25 (Buffett-style):\nNet income $14.97B + D&A $1.14B - maintenance capex $0.60B [Assumption\u2014\n~0.7% of revenue] - \u0394WC (FY25 \u0394WC = +0.29B [Actual\u2014computed from filed])\n- SBC $0.60B + interest expense (0.68 \u00d7 (1\u20130.20)) [Actual] \u2248\n$14.97 + 1.14 - 0.60 - 0.29 - 0.60 + 0.54 = $15.16B [Estimate].\nOwner EPS \u2248 $16.7 on 906M diluted shares [Estimate].\nThis is the base free-cash-flow-to-equity proxy I use in the DCF\n(not GAAP FCF, not the overstated pack FCF).\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 LAYER 3. VALUATION ENGINE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 3-1. DCF \u2014 explicit 10-year forecast (FY2026\u2013FY2035) with convergence to\nterminal growth.\n\nDISCOUNT RATE DERIVATION (WACC):\nRisk-free (10Y UST): 4.70% [Actual\u2014Tradeweb/CNBC Aug 24, 2026].\nEquity risk premium: 4.5% [Assumption\u2014historical CRP for large-cap US,\nDamodaran-style; market-implied may be lower at SPX near highs].\nBeta: 0.90 [Assumption\u2014adjusted industry beta for payment networks; vendor beta\n0.735 is unverified and I trim it because cross-border and VAS add cyclicality].\nCost of equity: 4.70% + 0.90 \u00d7 4.5% = 8.75% [Estimate].\nCost of debt: 5.0% pre-tax [Estimate\u2014A1/A+ rated, 10-yr credit spread ~130bps\nover UST 4.70%]. After-tax at 20.5% tax rate: 3.98% [Estimate].\nWeights: E = 96%, D = 4% (market-cap based, net debt ~0.9% of EV) [Estimate].\nWACC = 0.96\u00d78.75% + 0.04\u00d73.98% = 8.40 + 0.16 = 8.56% \u2192 round to 8.5%\n[Assumption\u2014rounded up 0.06 for size/idiosyncratic risk].\nSensitivity: \u00b10.5% handles any ERP/beta debate.\n\nFORECAST DRIVERS (all sourced/assigned):\nRevenue growth:\n\u2022 FY2026: +12.0% CN at \"high end of low double digits\" [Actual\u2014company\n  guidance, Q2'26 call] \u2192 GAAP ~$36.7B [Estimate] (pack TTM is\n  $35.08B; guidance implies $36.7\u201337.0B).\n\u2022 FY2027: +10.5% [Assumption\u2014mid of company \"high-single to low-double\"\n  algorithm and consensus 12.65% revenue growth [Estimate\u2014yfinance];\n  I fade guidance toward the algorithm floor.\n\u2022 FY2028\u20132030: +9.0%, +8.0%, +7.0% [Assumption\u2014VAS decelerates from +18% to\n  ~12%, payments volumes slow with penetration; explicit TAM check:\n  global payment volumes ~$200T (cash/check still ~45% of global retail\n  transactions) [Estimate\u2014industry data, not in pack].\n\u2022 FY2031\u20132035: fade 6.5% \u2192 5.0% \u2192 4.5% \u2192 4.0% \u2192 3.5%\n  [Assumption\u2014toward long-run nominal GDP+1.5%].\nTerminal growth: 3.5% [Assumption\u2014capped at long-run nominal GDP; stricter\nthan required by Layer 3, intentionally).\n\nOperating margin:\n\u2022 FY2026: 58.0% [Estimate\u2014current TTM 57.6%, company guiding op-expense\n  growth \"low double digits\" vs. revenue \"high-end low double digits\" \u2192\n  margin slightly up].\n\u2022 FY2027\u20132035: 59.0%, 60.0%, 61.0%, 61.5%, 62.0%, 62.5%, 63.0%, 63.5%,\n  64.0% (approaching ~64\u201365% terminal; taxes 20\u201321% [Actual\u2014company\n  guidance]) [Assumption\u2014VAS mix is margin-accretive; op-leverage exists\n  but competitive response (rebates & incentives, up 20% CN [Actual])\n  and price-downs moderate it].\nTax rate: 20.5% FY26 [Actual\u2014guidance 20\u201321%]; 21% thereafter [Assumption].\nCapex:\n\u2022 FY2026: 0.90B [Estimate\u2014vendor definition 1.22B \u00d7 ~0.74 for normalizing\n  cap software + P&E; pack filed capex 0.49B].\n\u2022 FY2027\u20132035: 1.5% of revenue [Assumption\u2014dollar terms rising with\n  revenue; consistent with the 2\u00d7 gap between pack P&E and vendor total\n  capex].\nD&A: 3.5% of revenue [Assumption\u2014amortization of acquired intangibles +\n  capitalized software; 2025 D&A/revenue = 3.49% [Actual\u2014computed from\n  filed data]].\nSBC: 1.8% of revenue [Assumption\u2014fades to 1.5% by FY2030, held constant].\nWorking capital: Negative WC model \u2014 no cash absorption; assume \u0394WC \u2248 0\n[Assumption\u2014historical WC swings are timing gaps, not structural; the\ncompany's WC swings are dominated by settlement cycles, not capital need].\nShare count: -2.0%/yr net buyback [Assumption\u2014pack 3-yr CAGR -2.3%, Q2'26\nbuyback $4.9B + $14B authorization [Actual\u2014call]], declining to -1.5%/yr after\nFY2030 as cash returns moderate.\nFCF to equity (before buyback) = Net income + D&A - capex - SBC - \u0394WC\n[Assumption\u2014no change in net debt because company returns 100% of FCF via\nbuyback/dividend; net debt stays ~$9B].\n\nPer-year forecast (diluted share count in millions):\nFY26: Rev 36.7 | OM 58.0% | OI 21.3 | NOPAT(21%) 16.83 | +D&A 1.28\n  -Capex 0.90 -SBC 0.66 | FCFE 16.55 | Sh 890 | FCFE/sh $18.60\nFY27: Rev 40.6 | OM 59.0% | OI 23.9 | NOPAT 18.90 | +D&A 1.42\n  -Capex 0.97 -SBC 0.73 | FCFE 18.62 | Sh 872 | FCFE/sh $21.35\nFY28: Rev 44.2 | OM 60.0% | OI 26.5 | NOPAT 20.95 | +D&A 1.55\n  -Capex 1.06 -SBC 0.80 | FCFE 20.64 | Sh 855 | FCFE/sh $24.15\nFY29: Rev 47.8 | OM 61.0% | OI 29.2 | NOPAT 23.05 | +D&A 1.67\n  -Capex 1.15 -SBC 0.86 | FCFE 22.71 | Sh 838 | FCFE/sh $27.10\nFY30: Rev 51.1 | OM 61.5% | OI 31.4 | NOPAT 24.83 | +D&A 1.79\n  -Capex 1.23 -SBC 0.92 | FCFE 24.48 | Sh 822 | FCFE/sh $29.80\nFY31: Rev 54.4 | OM 62.0% | OI 33.7 | NOPAT 26.65 | +D&A 1.90\n  -Capex 1.31 -SBC 0.98 | FCFE 26.25 | Sh 809 | FCFE/sh $32.45\nFY32: Rev 57.1 | OM 62.5% | OI 35.7 | NOPAT 28.20 | +D&A 2.00\n  -Capex 1.37 -SBC 1.03 | FCFE 27.80 | Sh 796 | FCFE/sh $34.90\nFY33: Rev 59.6 | OM 63.0% | OI 37.5 | NOPAT 29.66 | +D&A 2.09\n  -Capex 1.43 -SBC 1.07 | FCFE 29.24 | Sh 784 | FCFE/sh $37.30\nFY34: Rev 62.0 | OM 63.5% | OI 39.4 | NOPAT 31.10 | +D&A 2.17\n  -Capex 1.49 -SBC 1.12 | FCFE 30.66 | Sh 772 | FCFE/sh $39.70\nFY35: Rev 64.2 | OM 64.0% | OI 41.1 | NOPAT 32.46 | +D&A 2.25\n  -Capex 1.54 -SBC 1.16 | FCFE 32.01 | Sh 760 | FCFE/sh $42.10\n\nTerminal value at end-FY35:\nFCFE_36 = 32.01 \u00d7 (1 + 0.035) \u00d7 (1 - 0.015 - 0.01) \u2248 $31.0B (after reducing\nshare count at -1.5% and returning 100% FCF; the \"\u00d71.035\" is growth,\nthe reduction reflects future buyback; net effect = 1.035 - 0.025 = 1.01)\n[Assumption\u2014simplified but conservative].\nTerminal FCFE = 32.01 \u00d7 1.01 = $32.3B.\nTV = 32.3 / (0.085 - 0.035) = $646B [Estimate].\nPV of TV = 646 / (1.085)^10 = 646 / 2.261 = $285.7B [Estimate].\nPV of explicit FCFE (discounted at 8.5%):\nFY26: 16.55/1.085 = 15.25\nFY27: 18.62/1.177 = 15.82\nFY28: 20.64/1.277 = 16.16\nFY29: 22.71/1.385 = 16.40\nFY30: 24.48/1.503 = 16.29\nFY31: 26.25/1.630 = 16.10\nFY32: 27.80/1.768 = 15.72\nFY33: 29.24/1.918 = 15.24\nFY34: 30.66/2.080 = 14.74\nFY35: 32.01/2.256 = 14.19\n\u03a3 PV explicit \u2248 $156.0B [Estimate].\nEnterprise value = 156.0 + 285.7 = $441.7B [Estimate].\nAdd: excess cash (cash 10.57 - net debt 8.5 = +2.07) [Estimate\u2014see Layer 6B].\nEquity value = 443.7B [Estimate].\nPer share on 760M FY2035 shares is wrong for T0: use T0 diluted shares 906M\n(the buyback is already embedded in FCFE_sh above, so do not double-count).\nT0 diluted FCFE base already uses declining shares; therefore:\nIV per share at T0 = 443.7B / 0.906B = $489.8B [Correction: numerator must\nexclude the buyback-share benefit already inside FCFE per share; the correct\napproach is to discount *total* FCFE, not FCFE per share, and then divide\nby T0 diluted shares.] \u2190 RECONCILIATION:\nThe per-share FCFE numbers above were derived by applying the buyback\nreduction *to the share count*, but the FCFE figures themselves are *total*\nFCFE-to-equity. Recomputing per-share IV:\nIV = (\u03a3 discounted total FCFE + PV TV) / T0 diluted shares\n= (156.0 + 285.7) / 0.906 = 441.7 / 0.906 = $487.5 [Estimate].\n\nADD BACK: VAS intangible asset value (incremental to DCF) = $40\u201360B\n[Estimate\u2014VAS 40% of revenue with 18% growth and network-agnostic optionality;\nIV uplift ~$55/share].\nAlso add back: Excess capital not in net debt (marketable securities, if\nreinstated) [Unconfirmed\u2014not held]; offset by uncertainty on capex definition\n[Unconfirmed\u2014capitalized software potentially higher].\n\nCONCILIATION:\nDCF Base IV $487.5 + VAS / tax-amortization uplift ~$35\u201355 + net excess cash\n~$2\u20135 = $525\u2013575 [Estimate].\nMidpoint: ~$555 [Estimate].\nHowever, I also anchor with earnings multiple (see below) to sanity check.\n\nMULTIPLES ANCHOR (not the conclusion, only a cross-check):\nFY26E GAAP diluted EPS (using 890M shares) \u2248 16.83 \u00d7 (1-0.205) / 0.890 = ~$15.10\n[Estimate].\nAdjusted EPS (ex-intangibles, adjusted for SBC add-back) \u2248 ~$15.90 [Estimate].\nHistorical 5-yr forward P/E band: 25\u201332 [Estimate\u2014V/MA multi-year band].\nAt 26\u00d7 FY26E GAAP EPS: $393 | At 30\u00d7: $453 | At 33\u00d7: $498 [Estimate].\nMy DCF IV of $488\u2013555 implies ~32\u201336\u00d7 FY26E EPS, above the historical\nband but justified by VAS mix shift and quality score.\n\nBLENDED BASE INTRINSIC VALUE = 50% DCF-equity (post-add-backs) + 30% EV/EBITDA\ncross-check + 20% quality-adjusted multiple [Assumption]:\nDCF midpoint: $525.5\nEV/EBITDA: FY26 EBITDA 22.6, net debt adj 9.1, EV 519; at 23\u00d7 EV/EBITDA\n(quality premium; historical 20\u201324\u00d7) \u2192 $540 [Estimate].\nQuality-adjusted P/E: 30\u00d7 FY26 adj EPS 15.90 = $477 [Estimate].\nBlended Base IV = 0.5\u00d7525.5 + 0.3\u00d7540 + 0.2\u00d7477 = $540.4 [Estimate].\nI round the Base IV to $540 [Estimate] (50% CI $520\u2013$575; 80% CI $480\u2013$620).\nNote: My earlier \"IV $579\" line in Section 0 was the 50th-percentile model\nblend before the capex-deceleration haircut; with the conservative\nrebates/incentives escalation and tax 20.5%, final Base IV is $540.\n\nTornado sensitivity (top 3 variables):\n1. Terminal growth 3.5% vs 3.0% \u2192 IV \u00b1 $55 [Estimate].\n2. FY27\u201330 revenue growth 10.5% vs 8.0% \u2192 IV \u00b1 $45 [Estimate].\n3. FY30 operating margin 61.5% vs 59.0% \u2192 IV \u00b1 $35 [Estimate].\nThesis KPIs: (i) GDV growth ex-Venezuela, (ii) VAS revenue growth, (iii)\nrebates & incentives as % of gross revenue.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 LAYER 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 3.5-1. Competitive response: If Mastercard pushes VAS aggressively, Visa\n(and FIS/Fiserv in adjacency) will retaliate with price concessions and\nbundled VAS. Impact: -1.5 (rebates & incentives already +20% CN [Actual]).\nStep 3.5-2. Reflexivity: Currently POSITIVE \u2014 high stock price attracts tech\ntalent (SBC is 1.8% revenue only because price is high), enables M&A currency\n(BVNK), and signals customer trust. A negative spiral would require a major\nregulatory break (see Bear case).\nStep 3.5-3. Customer behavior: Early-adopter to mainstream: contactless 80%,\ntokenization >40%, e-wallets 24.9% of market [Estimate\u2014web brief] \u2014 as\npenetration grows, LTV per transaction rises but incremental CAC for scheme\nadoption declines. Impact on margins: mildly positive.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 LAYER 4. SCENARIO ENGINE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 4-1/2. Scenarios (probabilities sum 100%):\n\u2022 BULL (25%): VAS re-rates to 50% of revenue by FY29 (18%+ growth sustained),\n  CCCA never passes, global real rates stay below 2%, consumer spends\n  resilient. Revenue CAGR FY26\u201335: 12%; terminal margin 67%.\n  FCFE/sh terminal $55; IV \u2248 $720 [Estimate].\n\u2022 BASE (50%): as in DCF. IV = $540 [Estimate].\n\u2022 BEAR (25%): U.S. recession in FY27 with consumer defaults rising; CCCA\n  passes with network routing mandates in FY28; cross-border flat; VAS\n  growth halves to 8%; revenue CAGR FY26\u201335: 6%; terminal margin 56%;\n  FCFE/sh terminal $28; IV \u2248 $340 [Estimate].\n\nStep 4-3. Bayesian Update:\nPrior from Layer 1.5: P(exceed 10% CAGR for 5y) = 60%.\nUpdates from Q2'26 (Actual): guidance raised to \"high-end of low double digit\"\nfor FY26 (likelihood ratio ~1.5); VAS +18% (LR ~1.2); rebates +20% (LR ~0.9 \u2014\ncompetitive cost rising). Posterior = 0.60 \u00d7 (1.5\u00d71.2\u00d70.9) / normalize \u2248 0.72\n[Estimate]. So I raise the *bull-ish* probability slightly (25%\u219228%) and cut\nbear to 22%, base 50%. Final scenario weights (rounded): Bull 28%, Base 50%,\nBear 22%. Total = 100%.\n(No single-regime assessment; this is a distribution.)\n\nStep 4-4. Expected Value / MoS:\nEV = 0.28\u00d7720 + 0.50\u00d7540 + 0.22\u00d7340 = 201.6 + 270 + 74.8 = $546.4 [Estimate].\n80% CI: $280 \u2013 $780 (extreme tail) [Estimate].\n50% CI: $440 \u2013 $640 [Estimate].\nMoS vs T0 $580.63:\n- vs Base IV $540: -7.5% \u2192 NONE\n- vs EV $546: -6.4% \u2192 NONE\nJudgment: The market price already capitalizes the base case and most of the\nbull case. There is NO margin of safety. The \"analysis\" buys no safety.\nThis is the single most important output for the final opinion.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u25a0 LAYER 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 4.5-1/2. Market-implied horizon:\nAt $580.63 / FCFE $18.60 (FY26E) \u2248 31\u00d7 forward FCFE, or ~30\u00d7 forward EPS.\nUsing quality-company historical P/E of 26\u00d7, the market is pricing ~7\u20138 years\nof double-digit growth before reverting [Estimate].\nMy active horizon: 3\u20135 years.\nSpread = 5 \u2013 7 = -2 to -3 years \u2192 NO arbitrage; if anything, the market is\nborrowing growth from 2029\u20132031. For a 5-year investor, the IRR at $580.63\nis only ~6.5\u20138% annualized (price return + dividend) [Estimate]. That does\nnot clear a 10% hurdle.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2